-- UKMLA Geriatric Medicine batch 1
-- 50 questions: continence & functional decline, delirium & dementia,
-- falls & frailty, osteoporosis, polypharmacy (10 each)

  'draft', 'foundation',
  'An 82-year-old woman presents with a 3-day history of increased urinary frequency, urgency, and new urge incontinence. Her daughter reports she has become more confused and unsteady over the same period. She has type 2 diabetes and mild vascular dementia. Observations: temperature 37.9C, HR 98 bpm, BP 128/78 mmHg. Urinalysis shows leucocytes 3+, nitrites positive, and no ketones.',
  'What is the most appropriate immediate management?',
  array['UTI','delirium','incontinence']
),
('c9000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000002', 'ukmla', '1721554b-3911-4ef1-930a-66ee8306d7da',
  'draft', 'foundation',
  'A 78-year-old man with benign prostatic hyperplasia presents to the emergency department with severe suprapubic pain and an inability to pass urine for the past 8 hours. He took an over-the-counter cold remedy containing an antihistamine and decongestant two days ago. On examination, there is a palpable, tender suprapubic mass. Bladder scan shows 900 mL of retained urine.',
  'What is the most appropriate immediate management?',
  array['acute urinary retention','BPH','catheterisation']
),
('c9000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000003', 'ukmla', '1721554b-3911-4ef1-930a-66ee8306d7da',
  'draft', 'core',
  'An 84-year-old woman in a nursing home develops new faecal incontinence with frequent small-volume loose stool over the past week. She has a history of chronic constipation and takes co-codamol for osteoarthritis. On digital rectal examination, a large mass of hard stool is palpable in the rectum. Abdominal examination reveals a palpable, non-tender mass in the left iliac fossa.',
  'What is the most likely explanation for her new incontinence?',
  array['overflow incontinence','faecal impaction','opioid-induced constipation']
),
('c9000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000004', 'ukmla', '1721554b-3911-4ef1-930a-66ee8306d7da',
  'draft', 'foundation',
  'A 79-year-old woman is admitted with a chest infection and, after 10 days as an inpatient, is now medically fit for discharge. Prior to admission she was independently mobile and lived alone, but she is now requiring assistance to transfer from bed to chair and has reduced grip strength. Her family are keen for her to go home today.',
  'What is the most appropriate next step before discharge?',
  array['functional decline','deconditioning','comprehensive geriatric assessment']
),
('c9000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000005', 'ukmla', '1721554b-3911-4ef1-930a-66ee8306d7da',
  'draft', 'core',
  'A 74-year-old woman with mild cognitive impairment reports several months of urgency and urge incontinence, occurring multiple times daily and disrupting her sleep. Urinalysis is negative for infection. She has already tried scheduled toileting and bladder training with limited benefit. Her carer asks about starting oxybutynin.',
  'What is the most important consideration before prescribing this medication?',
  array['urge incontinence','antimuscarinics','anticholinergic burden']
),
('c9000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000006', 'ukmla', '1721554b-3911-4ef1-930a-66ee8306d7da',
  'draft', 'foundation',
  'An 88-year-old woman with an indwelling urinary catheter, inserted six weeks ago for chronic retention, is reviewed in clinic. She is asymptomatic and systemically well. Routine urinalysis shows cloudy urine with leucocytes and nitrites positive.',
  'What is the most appropriate management?',
  array['catheter-associated bacteriuria','asymptomatic bacteriuria']
),
('c9000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000007', 'ukmla', '1721554b-3911-4ef1-930a-66ee8306d7da',
  'draft', 'core',
  'An 86-year-old woman is admitted with a hip fracture and is expected to have prolonged bed rest before mobilising. She is thin, has reduced oral intake, and her skin over the sacrum appears intact but she has been assessed as high risk on a pressure ulcer risk assessment tool.',
  'What is the most important initial measure to prevent pressure ulcer development?',
  array['pressure ulcer prevention','immobility','risk assessment']
),
('c9000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000008', 'ukmla', '1721554b-3911-4ef1-930a-66ee8306d7da',
  'draft', 'core',
  $x$A 91-year-old man with moderate Alzheimer's dementia, normally continent, is brought to the emergency department by his care home with a two-day history of new urinary incontinence and increasing agitation. He has not opened his bowels for five days. He is afebrile with normal observations. Abdominal examination reveals mild suprapubic fullness. Bladder scan shows a residual volume of 650 mL.$x$,
  'What is the most likely explanation for his new incontinence?',
  array['urinary retention','chronic retention with overflow','constipation']
),
('c9000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000009', 'ukmla', '1721554b-3911-4ef1-930a-66ee8306d7da',
  'draft', 'foundation',
  'A 76-year-old woman describes small-volume urine leakage that occurs when she coughs, laughs or lifts shopping bags, without a preceding urge to void. She has had three vaginal deliveries and is otherwise well, with a negative urinalysis.',
  'What is the most likely diagnosis?',
  array['stress incontinence','pelvic floor']
),
('c9000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000010', 'ukmla', '1721554b-3911-4ef1-930a-66ee8306d7da',
  'draft', 'core',
  'An 83-year-old man is being discharged home after a two-week admission for pneumonia. He lives alone in a first-floor flat with no lift, previously mobilised independently with a stick, but now requires supervision to walk short distances and has not yet managed stairs on the ward. His family live overseas and he has no other support at home.',
  'What is the most appropriate step before finalising his discharge plan?',
  array['discharge planning','functional assessment','home safety']
),
('c9000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000011', 'ukmla', 'b59e166a-97e0-42b7-9696-e88312c906a2',
  'draft', 'foundation',
  'An 85-year-old woman is three days post-operative following surgical fixation of a hip fracture. The nursing staff report that she has become withdrawn, drowsy during the day, and slow to respond to questions, which is different from her usual alert self. She was previously independent with no cognitive concerns. Observations are stable and blood tests show no acute abnormality apart from a mildly raised CRP.',
  'What is the most appropriate initial assessment tool to use?',
  array['delirium','hypoactive delirium','4AT']
),
('c9000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000012', 'ukmla', 'b59e166a-97e0-42b7-9696-e88312c906a2',
  'draft', 'foundation',
  'An 80-year-old man is admitted with acute confusion that developed over the past 24 hours, fluctuating between periods of drowsiness and agitation, with poor attention on assessment. His daughter reports he was fully independent and cognitively intact one week ago. He has a background of type 2 diabetes and is on metformin.',
  'Which feature most strongly favours a diagnosis of delirium over dementia?',
  array['delirium','dementia','differentiation']
),
('c9000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000013', 'ukmla', 'b59e166a-97e0-42b7-9696-e88312c906a2',
  'draft', 'core',
  'A 79-year-old man presents with a two-year history of gradually worsening memory loss, difficulty managing his finances, and getting lost on familiar routes. His wife reports personality has remained largely unchanged and he has no motor symptoms. MRI brain shows generalised cerebral atrophy with disproportionate hippocampal involvement.',
  'What is the most likely diagnosis?',
  array[$x$Alzheimer's disease$x$,'dementia subtype']
),
('c9000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000014', 'ukmla', 'b59e166a-97e0-42b7-9696-e88312c906a2',
  'draft', 'core',
  'A 74-year-old man with a long history of alcohol excess is admitted to a surgical ward for an elective hernia repair. On the third post-operative day, having had no alcohol since admission, he becomes acutely agitated, tremulous, diaphoretic, and reports seeing insects crawling on the walls. Observations show HR 118 bpm, BP 162/94 mmHg, temperature 37.8C.',
  'What is the most appropriate immediate management?',
  array['alcohol withdrawal','delirium tremens']
),
('c9000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000015', 'ukmla', 'b59e166a-97e0-42b7-9696-e88312c906a2',
  'draft', 'core',
  'An 87-year-old woman with advanced dementia is admitted to a medical ward and becomes increasingly agitated at night, attempting to climb out of bed and shouting. Non-pharmacological measures (reorientation, consistent staff, reducing noise, ensuring glasses and hearing aids are worn) have already been tried without success, and she is now at high risk of falling and pulling out her intravenous line.',
  'What is the most appropriate next step?',
  array['agitation','dementia','antipsychotic use']
),
('c9000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000016', 'ukmla', 'b59e166a-97e0-42b7-9696-e88312c906a2',
  'draft', 'core',
  'A 76-year-old man is brought to clinic by his wife, who describes fluctuating episodes of confusion over several months, with some days he seems entirely normal and others he is markedly muddled. He has also described seeing small animals in the room that are not there, and has developed slowness of movement and a resting tremor over the same period. He had an adverse reaction with severe rigidity when previously given an antipsychotic for agitation.',
  'What is the most likely diagnosis?',
  array['Lewy body dementia','visual hallucinations','parkinsonism']
),
('c9000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000017', 'ukmla', 'b59e166a-97e0-42b7-9696-e88312c906a2',
  'draft', 'foundation',
  'A 90-year-old woman with known mild dementia is admitted with acute confusion, agitation and reduced oral intake over the past two days. She is also complaining of increased urinary frequency and lower abdominal discomfort. Urinalysis shows leucocytes and nitrites positive, and she is afebrile with stable observations.',
  'What is the most appropriate initial approach to her new confusion?',
  array['delirium','UTI','reversible causes']
),
('c9000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000018', 'ukmla', 'b59e166a-97e0-42b7-9696-e88312c906a2',
  'draft', 'core',
  'A 79-year-old woman with moderate dementia and a background of recurrent falls is admitted with a chest infection. She is refusing intravenous antibiotics, stating she does not want any treatment. When assessed, she is unable to explain in her own words why treatment might help her, cannot recall the conversation minutes later, and is unable to weigh up the risks and benefits of refusing treatment.',
  'What is the most appropriate next step?',
  array['mental capacity','best interests','dementia']
),
('c9000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000019', 'ukmla', 'b59e166a-97e0-42b7-9696-e88312c906a2',
  'draft', 'advanced',
  'A hospital is implementing a quality improvement project to reduce the incidence of delirium on care of the elderly wards. The team wishes to introduce an evidence-based, multicomponent intervention targeting modifiable risk factors in high-risk inpatients such as those with visual/hearing impairment, dehydration, immobility, sleep disturbance and polypharmacy.',
  'Which approach is most likely to reduce the incidence of delirium in this population?',
  array['delirium prevention','multicomponent intervention']
),
('c9000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000020', 'ukmla', 'b59e166a-97e0-42b7-9696-e88312c906a2',
  'draft', 'core',
  $x$A 72-year-old woman presents with a three-month history of low mood, poor appetite, disturbed sleep and difficulty concentrating, which she and her family describe as 'forgetfulness'. On cognitive testing she frequently responds 'I don't know' and makes little effort to answer, though when prompted and encouraged she can often recall the correct answer. She has a past history of depression and recently lost her husband.$x$,
  'What is the most likely explanation for her cognitive symptoms?',
  array['pseudodementia','depression','cognitive impairment']
),
